WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:04.980
Am I too tall?

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.040
For most of its history,
America's population has been younger

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:12.540
than other nations. As a result, America
has been a nation of risk takers who

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.470
have quickly found prosperity. But in
recent years, an aging America — led

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:21.000
mostly by the baby boomers — has made life
more difficult for young Americans. As a

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.230
result, American institutions have
stagnated. A population that is aging is

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.560
not necessarily alarming. But with age
come more invasive and burdensome

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.420
regulations in many walks of life. In the
cases of housing and employment, older

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.230
Americans have slowly created policies
that protect their investments, whether

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:41.250
it be in property or a career. Stringent
zoning laws and employment credentialing

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:45.239
have caused younger Americans to spend
more time getting dubiously-useful

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:49.020
degrees and certifications, and taking on
more debt, delaying marriage and

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.629
childbearing, which in turn is making
America even older and more bureaucratic.

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:58.739
Simply put, when the population becomes
less agile, so do its foundational

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:03.510
institutions. Eliminating unnecessary
regulations and barriers in many sectors

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.360
would create an America that is stronger
and more prosperous for everyone.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.780
So what do you think about this? Do you think
America's institutions have become more

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:15.450
rigid and inflexible? Let us know in our
poll. Also, let us know what other topics

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.869
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60
seconds, and be sure to like and

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:23.390
subscribe for more research and videos
from AEI.

